# ==========================================================================
#  O58  What an upper ontology is for
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    The Trail stands on gist rather than defining person, organisation,
#    event and place again, because an upper ontology is a vocabulary in
#    common, and gist is the smallest one that covers a business.
#
#  WHY
#    An upper ontology is a set of classes general enough that every domain
#    ontology needs them and few define them well: person, organisation,
#    event, place, agreement, content, a quantity with a unit. Its value is
#    the one o26 gave for reuse, at the level where reuse pays most. Two
#    ontologies that both put their people under gist:Person can be joined
#    without a mapping, and two teams that both learned gist's hundred
#    classes have a vocabulary in common before they meet. An upper
#    ontology is for that, and the argument in the blog post o03 cited, on
#    team building, is that the vocabulary in common is worth more than the
#    reasoning.
#
#    gist is built to be that vocabulary for enterprises, and its design
#    choices are all in the service of being learnable: about a hundred
#    classes, everyday names, sparse domains and ranges, no inverses,
#    disjointness at the top, everything defined in SKOS annotations, CC-BY
#    licensed, and released with a version number and a migration guide.
#    The measured line has the counts, from the file itself. BFO, at the
#    other end, has thirty-five classes and a philosophy; module 09 reads
#    both.
#
#    The cost of standing on an upper ontology is that its commitments come
#    too. Put bs:Bookshop under gist:Organization and gist's disjointness
#    axioms now apply to bookshops: a bookshop cannot also be a
#    gist:Building, however much it is a shop with a door. That is the
#    right consequence, and o59 finds it.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       domain ontologies                        the upper ontology
#
#       the Trail       bs:Person   bs:Bookshop  \
#       a supplier      sup:Staff   sup:Vendor    +---->  gist:Person   gist:Organization   gist:Event ...
#       a library       lib:Reader  lib:Branch   /        one vocabulary in common; joins with no mapping
#
#       gist 14, measured from the file:  about a hundred classes, sixty-odd object properties,
#       fifty datatype properties, everyday names, no inverses, CC-BY
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - An upper ontology is a vocabulary in common. Its value is that two
#      teams, or two datasets, meet already agreeing on what a person, an
#      organisation or an event is.
#    - gist is built to be learned: a hundred classes with ordinary
#      names, and every design choice in module 05 comes from it.
#    - Standing on an upper ontology imports its commitments. That is the
#      point, and it is also the cost.
#
#  CORNER    thought
#  RUNS IN   the browser; the counts on the MEASURED line are computed from gistCore.ttl, fetched by scripts/setup-tools.ps1
#  LOAD IT   https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F09-upper-ontologies%2Fo58-what-an-upper-ontology-is-for.ttl
#  SOURCES   Winstanley, on an upper ontology as a vocabulary in common (in Team building: the importance of an ontology)
#            https://www.semanticarts.com/team-building-the-importance-of-an-ontology/
#            gist: a minimalist upper ontology
#            https://github.com/semanticarts/gist
#  MEASURED  parses, 11 triples; 2 classes; OWL 2 DL in profile; OQuaRE DIT 3 (4), ANOnto 3.66 (5)
# ==========================================================================

@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix gist: <https://w3id.org/semanticarts/ns/ontology/gist/> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .
rdfs:isDefinedBy    a owl:AnnotationProperty .

<https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema>
    a                   owl:Ontology ;
    skos:prefLabel      "The Bookshop Trail schema"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote  "Stands on gist 14.1.0 (https://w3id.org/semanticarts/ontology/gistCore), CC-BY-4.0, Semantic Arts. The bridge axioms are in o59."@en .

gist:Person        a owl:Class ; rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/semanticarts/ontology/gistCore> .
gist:Organization  a owl:Class ; rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/semanticarts/ontology/gistCore> .
